From: Adriel Santos <adriel@adriel.eu>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: keep the HAL when crash recovery fails

ath11k_core_reconfigure_on_crash() keeps the HAL across a restart
(ath11k_hal_srng_clear() instead of deinit/init, since 32be3ca4cf78b),
but when ath11k_core_qmi_firmware_ready() fails it still calls
ath11k_hal_srng_deinit(), which frees the rdp/wrp buffers and sets
srng_config to NULL. Nothing initialises the HAL again: the next restart
of that device goes through ath11k_hal_srng_clear() and
ath11k_ce_init_pipes(), which dereference the freed state
(ath11k_hal_srng_get_entrysize() reads srng_config).

This is reachable on an IPQ5018 when the firmware crashes again during
recovery ("failed to start core: -110", "failed to reconfigure driver on
crash recovery") and the device is then restarted, e.g. by a watchdog
restarting its user PD.

Keep the HAL on failure; it is freed on device removal as usual. A
failed recovery can leave it half set up, so also make the paths that
run before the next restart cope with that:

- set ATH11K_FLAG_CRASH_FLUSH again, so nothing is sent through the
  rings; the next restart clears it before setting them up.

- ath11k_ce_tx_process_cb() is reached from ath11k_ce_cleanup_pipes()
  and ath11k_ce_send() before that check. Skip it while the pipe's
  source ring is not set up (ath11k_hal_srng_clear() zeroed srng_list).
  This covers the TX completion poll only, which the next restart's
  ath11k_ce_cleanup_pipes() always runs; it does not make the window
  in which that restart sets the rings up again safe for concurrent
  senders, which was not safe before either.

- ath11k_dp_free() frees the TX status buffers without clearing their
  pointers; the next restart's ath11k_dp_free(), or device removal,
  would free them again if ath11k_dp_alloc() failed before reallocating
  them.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/core.c
@@ -2406,7 +2406,11 @@
 	return 0;
 
 err_hal_srng_deinit:
-	ath11k_hal_srng_deinit(ab);
+	/* Keep the HAL: the next restart clears and reuses it
+	 * (ath11k_hal_srng_clear() needs srng_config), and device removal
+	 * frees it. Nothing may send through the half-set-up rings meanwhile.
+	 */
+	set_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_CRASH_FLUSH, &ab->dev_flags);
 	return ret;
 }
 
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ce.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/ce.c
@@ -502,6 +502,13 @@
 	struct sk_buff *skb;
 	struct sk_buff_head list;
 
+	/* A crash recovery that failed early leaves the HAL ring table
+	 * cleared until the next one sets the rings up again.
+	 */
+	if (!pipe->src_ring ||
+	    !ab->hal.srng_list[pipe->src_ring->hal_ring_id].initialized)
+		return;
+
 	__skb_queue_head_init(&list);
 	while (!IS_ERR(skb = ath11k_ce_completed_send_next(pipe))) {
 		if (!skb)
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/dp.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/dp.c
@@ -1044,6 +1044,10 @@
 		kfree(dp->tx_ring[i].tx_status);
 	}
 
+	/* freed again if the next ath11k_dp_alloc() fails before replacing them */
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(dp->tx_ring); i++)
+		dp->tx_ring[i].tx_status = NULL;
+
 	/* Deinit any SOC level resource */
 }
 
